# Backend

## Architecture

- `/api` — media, tags, tag-groups, scrape, app; plus `/api/health`.
- Thin `app/api/`; logic in `services/` and `scraper/`.
- Settings: `data/app_settings.json` via `app_settings.py` (not `config.py`).
- Config: paths, scrape knobs, `phash_threshold` — no site URLs, no `blacklist_tags`.
- Extension: `api/extension.py` (`GET /extension/sites`, `POST /extension/saved`, `POST /extension/enqueue`, `POST /extension/scrape`, `POST /extension/save-file`). Sites come from the adapter `extension` block (`page_matches`, `post_id_patterns`, `post_url_template`, `file_matches`). `POST /extension/saved` returns the submitted URLs whose post id is already a library row; a CDN file counts as that post. `sites.clean_page_url` drops the query and fragment; `extension_scrape_target` turns a matching page into the URL the scraper runs (the post page itself, or the post page for a CDN preview). A running scrape of that site takes the URL via `pipeline.append_scrape_urls` (`total` grows, counters are not reset, `pending_urls` drained by `run_job`); otherwise `start_scrape_run` starts a URL-filter job. `/extension/scrape` starts that site's gallery with Scrape-page filters. `/extension/save-file` always `add_queue`s one http(s) picture first (no tags). When `never_auto_run_scrape` is on it returns that URL as queued and does not download. Otherwise `pipeline.begin_remote_download` starts, appends, or reports busy (`storage.save_remote_file`). **Stop** is not cleared in `_reset_status`; `_skip_cancelled_remainder` adds every file still left to skipped. Same `scrape_lock` as `/scrape/run`. Tests: `tests/test_extension.py`.
- Sites: `scraper/sites.py` catalog (`KNOWN_SITES` + `KNOWN_SCRAPERS` agents) + `data/session_settings.json` (`selected_site_id`, `selected_scraper_id`). A scraper can list multiple `site_ids`; the Scrape UI picks site then agent.
- Media: `media/{image|gif|video}/…` at `/media/...`.
- SPA: serve `frontend/dist`; never SPA-catch `/api/*`.
- `GET /media` filters/sorts/pages in SQL (no load-all + Python slice); hidden groups arrive as `exclude_tag_groups` keys and are expanded server-side together with aliases of hidden tags.
- `POST /media/bulk` (`{ids, action: discard|delete}`, 1–500 ids) runs in one transaction via `bulk_media_action`; files are unlinked only after commit. Returns `processed` / `missing` ids.
- Page responses use `build_media_summaries` (batched links / canonical aliases / ratings / source URLs). Never build a list by calling `build_media_summary` per item — that was ~1500 queries per page.
- `resolve_canonical_tags` is the batched form of `resolve_canonical_tag`; list endpoints (`GET /tags`) use it plus grouped usage-count / alias-name queries.
- `init_db()` must keep `_backfill_strip_unknown_when_grouped` and other backfills it calls.

## Tags / groups

- Soft-delete tags (`is_deleted`); revive by name on create — no duplicate rows.
- Default group for new tags; unknown dropped when another group is added.
- Multi-group: `tag_group_links` is the membership table; `Tag.group_id` is the primary (color / order scope). `+` uses `add_group_ids` (append); chip click uses `replace_group`; × uses `remove_group_ids`. Never treat `add_group_ids` as a primary-only overwrite.
- Group merge: bulk-update `group_id` + retarget links, densify islands, **core** DELETE sources (Unknown not a source).
- Order islands: contiguous `sort_order` derived on demand; insert/delete per island; same-island move = swap-shift (incl. after-last → last slot when not filling a gap to the next island); cross-island = lift+insert; drop-before island with room → `start-1` (joins); first-slot lift leaves hole; reset-by-name within islands.
- Island growth (densify of duplicates, insert, group merge) pushes the *following* island via `shift_to_clear` (round up to `ISLAND_GAP_BUMP`), keeping ≥1 empty slot. Never let a densified island overwrite its neighbour — that produced duplicate `sort_order`.
- Islands from `order_islands` are maximal, so neighbours always differ by ≥2: do not add "islands touch" checks, they cannot fire.
- Auto-tests: `scripts\run_order_tests.bat` / `tests/test_order_islands.py` after order or tag-service changes.
- Group order user-owned; palette one-way consume on create.

## Scrape / CDN

- Media database ids (`media_items.id`) use SQLite `AUTOINCREMENT` so a deleted id is never reused for a new file (preview cache and UI keys stay unique). Existing libraries are rebuilt once in `init_db._ensure_media_items_autoincrement`.
- Local import: `storage_kind` `managed`|`linked`; linked rows keep absolute `local_path`, synthetic `loc_<hex>` `source_post_id`, and an internal `file:///` `source_url` that is not returned or shown as a source URL. No tags. `POST /scrape/import/pick-folder` / `pick-files` (tkinter dialogs), `POST /scrape/import/run` shares `scrape_status` + `recent_media_ids`. Library delete skips unlinking linked files; `DELETE /media/{id}/local-file` copies the file into the library and deletes only the folder original. Serve linked (and by-id) via `GET /media/{id}/file`.
- Tag fetch: curl_cffi → httpx → curl → browser JSON → HTML. Direct `/post/{id}` seeds prefer `scrape_post_from_api` (JSON + CDN, no browser); browser HTML is fallback. `expand_tags` / Show more is best-effort (`no_wait_after`, never fails the post). `goto_post` retries transient SSL/network errors (Obscura stealth often returns BoringSSL `WRONG_VERSION_NUMBER` on the first handshake). Gallery listing uses the same helper, up to 6 attempts. The browser step uses `PlaywrightClient`, which opens Obscura (`serve --stealth` + CDP) or Playwright Chromium. `scrape_browser` is `auto` (Obscura if installed), `obscura`, or `playwright`. Engines live in `backend/scraper/browsers/`: shared page/context logic in `base.BrowserEngine`, one subclass per engine that only starts/stops the browser. A new engine = subclass + a branch in `session.open_engine`.
- Engines open one context per page and free it once the page is closed (pruned on the next `new_page`); the JSON-request context is created on first `request_json`. Never keep a context per page alive for the whole job — long scrapes leaked hundreds.
- Auto falls back to Playwright when Obscura fails to start: logged, and exposed as `fallback_error` on `GET /app/browsers`. Explicit `obscura` raises instead.
- A new outside app or library needs launcher install/update logic. Scrape-only tools follow the browser gate below: no probe, install, or update while `hide_scrape_page` is on. Library tools that run with the Scrape page hidden still install on launch, and are not behind that gate.
- Browsers exist only for scraping. While `hide_scrape_page` is on, `api/guards.require_scrape_page` returns 409 for `POST /scrape/run`, `/media/{id}/rescrape`, `/media/{id}/refresh-tags` and `/app/browsers/install`, and the launcher does not touch browsers.
- `catalog.py` / `install.py` stay stdlib-only at import time — the launcher imports them before app packages load. Missing browsers are never auto-installed: the Scrape page notice and Settings **Download** call `POST /app/browsers/install`. Obscura installs the latest GitHub release into `data/browsers/obscura/` (checksum from the release asset `digest`, version in `version.txt`); the pinned version + SHA-256 is the offline fallback (bump both together). Playwright **Download** first repairs a broken package (missing `driver/package/cli.js` or damaged dist-info via `--ignore-installed --no-deps`) then runs `playwright install chromium`. The Chromium path is probed once per process.
- `install.update_browsers` runs in the launcher before the server starts (replacing `obscura.exe` or the playwright package under a running scrape fails on Windows), at most once per 24 h (`data/browsers/update-check.json`, shown in Settings), and only when `check_browser_updates_on_start` is on. That checkbox is off by default when Windows reports a metered connection (`browsers/metered.py`, `INetworkCostManager`); once saved it stays as the user set it. It updates only installed browsers: the app-downloaded Obscura (an `OBSCURA_PATH`/PATH copy is left alone) and Playwright via `pip install --upgrade playwright` + `playwright install chromium` (Chromium builds are tied to the package). A damaged dist-info is overwritten with `--ignore-installed --no-deps`.
- Never overwrite a known tag group with another known/Unknown when site is untyped.
- Card previews: `GET /api/media/{id}/preview?size=compact|normal|large` (`preview_cache.ensure_preview`) writes a center-cropped JPEG (quality 62) at 240 / 360 / 480 px under `data/previews/{size}/{id}.jpg`, plus a stamp of the source size and mtime. The cache is rebuilt when either changes, including when a copied local file is older than a leftover card. Deleting the library row deletes those previews. Videos use one ffmpeg frame; a missing ffmpeg or unreadable file returns 404 and the card falls back to the original. Detail and `GET /media/{id}/file` still serve the full file. The response is `Cache-Control: public, max-age=86400` so the browser keeps the JPEG about a day (`v={file_size}` on the URL busts a replaced file).
- Gallery site JSON post `type` 0=image, 1=video; CDN via curl_cffi when httpx 403s. `storage.fetch_media_bytes` retries curl_cffi up to `MEDIA_FETCH_ATTEMPTS` (6) on TLS/connection errors (VPNs drop ~half of handshakes), then tries httpx; HTTP 4xx is not retried. Files are written via `.part` + rename. `get_scrape_concurrency()` (Settings → Other → Scrape, **Downloads at once**, default 2, clamped 1–6) downloads that many posts or raw files at once; each worker uses its own DB session, and the Playwright fallback stays on the listing thread. Listing stops enqueueing when downloaded + in-flight + queued cover the limit.
- Launcher (and `install.py` when it runs `playwright install`) strips the Cursor sandbox `PLAYWRIGHT_BROWSERS_PATH`.
- `ScrapeStatus.recent_media_ids` accumulates real downloads for the process lifetime (not cleared by `_reset_status` between jobs); exposed on status + `GET /scrape/recent-media?scope=session|all` / `POST /scrape/clear-recent`. `scope=all` pages the library by `scraped_at` desc. `POST /scrape/clear-queues[?source=key&which=queue|skipped|errors|cancelled]` calls `scrape_leftovers.clear_queues(source, which=)` (queue/unfinished/page_urls/sources; `open` only on a full clear; `which` drops one list — `skipped` excludes reason=error, `errors` clears reason=error only) and `scrape_panels.clear_panel` under `scrape_lock`; clearing Cancelled (or a full clear) also shrinks `total`/`processed` for those URLs so progress cannot stay e.g. 10/23 with empty queues; idle panels with no outstanding lists clamp `total` to `processed` on status read; 409 while `running` for that source (or any source when no `source`); never touches `recent_media_ids`. Job end (`_release_queue`) drops uncounted leftover `pending_urls` from `total` instead of leaving a gap. `POST /scrape/retry?source=key&which=skipped|errors` runs that panel's skipped and/or error files (`scrape_panels.retry_targets`); Queue to scrape and Cancelled stay. Failures use `error_items` (not `skipped_items`); with `never_auto_run_scrape` off the same job retries a failing file up to 3 times before Errors. Startup `restore_into_status` clears `recent_media_ids` and prior progress counters so Reload starts a fresh session list.
- Scrape panels: `services/scrape_panels.py` keeps one live job (`scrape_status`) but splits history by source. `scrape_status.source_key/source_label` own the live counters and lists; `activate()` (called under `scrape_lock` when Local import, a site scrape, or a raw download starts, before the job touches counters) parks the previous source's numbers and loads the new source's (or zeros). Keys: `local`, site id (label = site name), `unsupported`. `GET /scrape/status` adds `source_key`, `source_label`, and `sources: [ScrapeSourcePanelOut]` (counts, message, skipped_items, cancelled_urls, queue_urls, errors per source; sorted Local, sites, Unsupported). `scrape_leftovers` stores each URL's source (`sources` map for the queue, `source` on unfinished rows; a URL without one is guessed with `extension_scrape_target`, else `unsupported`); `add_queue(..., source=)`, `snapshot/unfinished_items/queue_urls(source)`, `queue_by_source()`. `POST /scrape/run` takes optional `source_key`: the selected site id (else 400), or `unsupported` (retries only its raw-file leftovers, 400 when none). Run on a site drains only that site's leftovers. `restore_into_status` / `scrape_panels.restore` clears prior-session progress + Downloaded previously, then spreads stored rows over panels (`processed` 0, `total` = leftover Queue + Skipped + Cancelled per panel; first source live, rest parked). `processed` counts every settled file (download, skip, error, cancel). A new run takes retried skipped and error URLs back out of `processed` until they settle; a successful download of a previous error removes it from Skipped, `scrape_status.errors`, and unfinished leftovers, and still counts once. Tests reset with `scrape_panels.reset(scrape_status)`.
- Gallery listing sort: expand Filters And Settings, then click Oldest/Latest before yielding URLs so asc+limit downloads oldest matches (not newest). Scrape pages block the gallery popunder script (`admaze` / `popunder`); if a click still leaves the site, the listing closes that popup and returns to the gallery before reading post links.
- Scrape `limit` = max **successful downloads** (skips/duplicates do not count). Status includes `current_url`, and `skipped_items: [{url, reason}, ...]` cleared on each new job.
- `duplicate_of_id` points later files at the earliest match. `duplicate_kind` is `identical` (image/gif pHash Hamming 0–2, or a video whose four ffmpeg frame hashes all fall in that band) or `similar` (pictures only, 3–sensitivity). SHA-256 hits skip the second file and copy tags + URL onto the kept row. `scrape_skip_identical` does the same for a new post in the identical band. Matcher lives in `hash_service` (`find_earlier_phash_match`, `classify_stored_hash`); groups/merge/hand-mark/keep in `duplicate_service`. Settings **Similar image sensitivity** is what the matcher uses (not `config.phash_threshold`). `GET /media/duplicates` returns `{identical, similar}` groups; `POST /media/duplicates/merge` unions tags/URLs/optional rating then discards the rest; `POST /media/duplicates/keep` unlinks the chosen ids and discards the other ids in `scope_ids`; `POST /media/{id}/related` hand-links local database ids to that file (earliest id in each pair stays primary); `POST /media/duplicates/mark` links a selection (earliest id stays primary); `GET /media/{id}/duplicate-group` feeds media detail.
